TimeDilation
============

TimeDilation is an adversarial sched_ext scheduler for race-condition
research.  It turns narrow TOCTOU and use-after-free windows into
stable, repeatable ones by running a target's racing threads on
dedicated per-CPU lanes and controlling exactly which other tasks may
run on those CPUs.  While a lane is armed the target's threads execute
in lockstep, and every competing task on the lane CPU is kept off it
until the window closes.

The scheduler is a struct_ops BPF program loaded through sched_ext.  A
small userspace tool exposes a command language for arming lanes,
setting per-thread slices, parking kernel workers, and isolating
cgroups.  All state lives in BPF maps pinned under
/sys/fs/bpf/timedilation, so a live scheduler can be inspected and
steered from a second process.


Requirements
------------

    Linux 6.12 or newer with CONFIG_SCHED_CLASS_EXT=y
    clang, llvm-strip, bpftool
    libbpf, libelf, zlib and pthread development headers
    root privileges to register a sched_ext scheduler

Kernel BTF must be available at /sys/kernel/btf/vmlinux.  The build
generates vmlinux.h from it, so the kernel headers do not have to be
installed system-wide.


Building
--------

    make

The Makefile drives the whole build:

    vmlinux.h           generated from /sys/kernel/btf/vmlinux
    TimeDilation.bpf.o  compiled from TimeDilation.bpf.c
    TimeDilation.skel.h generated by bpftool gen skeleton
    TimeDilation        userspace loader and control tool
    td_trigger_test     exerciser for the in-kernel auto-trigger

The same steps can be run by hand when make is unavailable:

    bpftool btf dump file /sys/kernel/btf/vmlinux format c > vmlinux.h
    clang -g -O2 -target bpf -D__TARGET_ARCH_x86 -I. \
          -c TimeDilation.bpf.c -o TimeDilation.bpf.o
    llvm-strip -g TimeDilation.bpf.o
    bpftool gen skeleton TimeDilation.bpf.o > TimeDilation.skel.h
    gcc -Wall -O2 -I. TimeDilation.c -o TimeDilation \
        -lbpf -lelf -lz -lpthread

Replace x86 with arm64 in BPF_CFLAGS when building on AArch64.

    make clean

removes the generated files and both binaries.


Environment
-----------

    TIMEDILATION_BPF_DIR

        Directory for the pinned BPF maps.  Defaults to
        /sys/fs/bpf/timedilation and is created (including missing
        parents) if it does not exist.  The directory must live on a
        bpffs mount; libbpf rejects pin paths on other filesystems.

        Set the same value for the loader, the control commands and
        td_trigger_test when they are meant to operate on the same
        loaded scheduler:

            sudo TIMEDILATION_BPF_DIR=/sys/fs/bpf/td-alt \
                ./TimeDilation load
            sudo TIMEDILATION_BPF_DIR=/sys/fs/bpf/td-alt \
                ./TimeDilation status

        If the path is not on bpffs, the loader prints a warning and
        continues without pinning: the scheduler runs, but status/arm
        commands and td_trigger_test cannot reach its maps.


Quick start
-----------

    sudo ./TimeDilation load

loads and attaches the scheduler and stays in the foreground; press
Ctrl+C to detach.  In another terminal, register the racing threads of
a target process on lane 0:

    sudo ./TimeDilation arm 0 4242,4243,4244

or, when the target is known by name:

    sudo ./TimeDilation arm -n 0 my_racer

arm -n matches the name against the comm of every task on the system
and registers each matching task id.  Only threads that are allowed to
run on the lane CPU should be registered; a thread pinned elsewhere
cannot migrate to the lane.

    sudo ./TimeDilation status

prints the per-lane state and the registered threads.  Remove the lane
with:

    sudo ./TimeDilation disarm

A single process can also load, attach and arm in one step:

    sudo ./TimeDilation load arm 0 4242,4243


Commands
--------

    load [ACTION...]
        Load and attach the scheduler; hold until interrupted.  An
        optional follow-up action runs immediately after attach.

    arm [-n] CORE PID[,PID...]
    arm [-n] CORE NAME[,NAME...]
        Register racing threads on lane CORE.  CORE must be an online
        CPU and inside the lane range 0-7 (TD_MAX_CORES).  With -n each
        argument is matched against the comm of every task and each
        matching task id is registered.

    disarm
        Disarm every lane and clear the thread registrations.

    slice PID:NS[,PID:NS...]
        Set a per-thread lane slice in nanoseconds.  NS=0 selects the
        default 5 ms slice.  The thread must already be registered.

    park CORE
    resume CORE
        Park kernel workers (hold_k=1) so that they are held off the
        lane CPU, then release them onto the lane (release_k=1).

    starve PID[,PID...]
    starve-off PID[,PID...]
        Route a task to the starvation DSQ or deactivate it again.

    starve-set N
        Set starvation intensity, 0 (off) through 4 (deny).

    cg-freeze CID[,CID...]
    cg-unfreeze CID[,CID...]
        Park all tasks in the given cgroups in the frozen DSQ, or
        release them.

    park-comm COMM[,COMM...]
    unpark-comm COMM[,COMM...]
        Restrict kernel-worker parking to the listed comm names.

    status
        Print lane state, registered threads and scheduler counters.

    help
        Print the built-in command summary.


Upgrade layer
-------------

Four bolt-on mechanisms extend the base lane scheduler.

    U1 - in-kernel auto-trigger

        Kprobes on the functions listed in TD_TRIGGER_LIST
        (TimeDilation.h) flip release_k=1 and frozen=1 on the arming
        core from inside the kernel.  The current list is
        io_uring_try_cancel_requests(), unix_gc(), unix_release_sock()
        and tcp_v4_syn_recv_sock().  Because no userspace round trip is
        involved, entry into a vulnerable code path is observed within
        tens of nanoseconds.

        The list is the only place probe targets are enumerated: one
        X(symbol) line expands into a kprobe program on the BPF side and
        an entry in the userspace attach table.  When a symbol does not
        exist on the running kernel, its attach fails with a warning and
        the remaining probes still load.

    U2 - finite slice at preemption points

        When U1 fires, the victim thread is given a short finite slice
        (TD_TRIGGER_SLICE_NS, 50 us) instead of an infinite one.  The
        thread is therefore preemptible at tick expiry and at
        cond_resched() and mutex points, which replaces a page-fault
        based descheduling primitive without generating faults.

    U3 - surgical kernel-worker filtering

        Kernel threads (PF_KTHREAD) are parked only when their comm
        matches an entry programmed through park-comm.  The match is
        performed in BPF with BPF_CORE_READ_STR_INTO.  Parked tasks
        trickle out at one task per 4000 ms, inside a 20 ms release
        window, so the scheduler's .timeout_ms watchdog is never
        tripped.  Unmatched kernel threads go to a per-CPU keepalive
        queue rather than being starved.

    U4 - starvation queue and cgroup isolation

        A starvation DSQ (DSQ_STARVE, 2001) mirrors the primitives from
        scx_starve: target tasks are consumed last with a small slice,
        according to the configured intensity.  A second DSQ
        (TD_DSQ_FROZEN, 902) holds every task whose cgroup id appears
        in the freeze list; those tasks are only trickled through the
        U3 heartbeat.  Cgroup ids come from bpf_get_current_cgroup_id().


How it works
------------

The scheduler keeps four kinds of queues:

    TD_DSQ_ID(core)     one race lane per CPU, 100 + core
    TD_DSQ_NORMAL       the default queue, 900
    TD_DSQ_KPARKED      parked kernel workers, 901
    TD_DSQ_FROZEN       cgroup-frozen tasks, 902
    TD_DSQ_KOTHER(cpu)  per-CPU keepalive queues, 910 + cpu
    TD_DSQ_STARVE       starvation queue, 2001

select_cpu() pins a registered thread to its lane CPU.  enqueue()
routes it into the lane DSQ with either the per-thread slice, the
trigger slice, or the 5 ms default.  dispatch() consumes only the lane
queue on an armed CPU, so no other task runs there while the race is
in flight.  This also preserves per-CPU SLUB locality: the spraying
and freeing sides of the race run on the CPU that owns the target
cache, with no migration or competing allocations in between.

Kernel workers are handled separately.  While hold_k is set they are
routed to TD_DSQ_KPARKED and released only when a lane is resumed;
release_k set by U1 releases them onto the lane for the duration of
the window.  The heartbeat keeps the scheduler responsive during long
holds.

Tasks that belong to a frozen cgroup, and tasks explicitly starved,
never enter a lane: they are parked in TD_DSQ_FROZEN or queued on
TD_DSQ_STARVE, which dispatch() consumes last.


Verifying a run
---------------

    sudo ./TimeDilation load

    sudo ./TimeDilation arm -n 0 my_racer

    ps -o pid,psr,comm -L -p $(pgrep -x my_racer)

The registered threads should report psr equal to the lane CPU and
should not migrate while the lane is armed.

    sudo ./TimeDilation status

reports the lane table and the counters below.  They confirm that the
lane, the auto-trigger and the heartbeat are actually engaging:

    nr_race_enqueued       threads routed into a lane
    nr_lane_dispatched     lane tasks dispatched locally
    nr_auto_triggered      U1 kprobe triggers fired
    nr_held_kthreads       kernel workers parked
    nr_kresumed            parked workers released
    nr_hb_trickles         U3 heartbeat releases
    nr_frozen_enqueued     U4 frozen-cgroup tasks parked
    nr_starved_enqueued    U4 tasks routed to DSQ_STARVE

The U1 path can be exercised without a target:

    sudo ./td_trigger_test

The binary sets up an AF_UNIX SCM_RIGHTS spray, which enters
unix_gc()/unix_release_sock() and should increment nr_auto_triggered.


Limitations
-----------

Lane arming affects only threads that can run on the lane CPU.  A
thread pinned to another CPU never reaches the lane.

CORE arguments are validated against /sys/devices/system/cpu/online
and the lane array, so an offline CPU or a lane above 7 is rejected.
On machines with more than eight CPUs only cores 0-7 receive lanes;
kernel threads on higher CPUs are queued on the normal DSQ instead of
a per-CPU keepalive queue.

Holding a lane for a long time deliberately starves background work on
that CPU.  The heartbeat bounds the damage for parked workers, but a
lane should still be disarmed once the race is over.

The scheduler requires a kernel with sched_ext and a BPF toolchain
matching the running kernel's BTF.  It is research tooling: expect to
reload it after load or stress failures, and treat it as unsuitable
for production systems.


Provenance
----------

TimeDilation builds on the adversarial scheduling research and the
scx_starve primitives published in:

    https://github.com/rootagi/scx_research

The lane scheduler, the U1-U4 mechanisms and the control tool in this
repository are self-contained and do not require a checkout of that
project.


